    "knowledge_point": "Vertical Reflections of Exponential Growth Functions",
    "problem": "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-{2}^{x}-4?$",
    "worked_solution": "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y={2}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938518/assets/2fe0fb3f0f5d302d39f6ad578148c4973618595452b59fea8a8bb2399ef92ddc.png)\n\n- Reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-{2}^{x}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,-1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938518/assets/98c622f4675812dcada993e4022398aac18f37cda76bbaf3ecbe6147c6e5bc97.png)\n\n- Then, vertically shift it by $4$ units down to give $y=-{2}^{x}-4.$ The point $(0,-1)$ moves to $(0,-5)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-4.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938518/assets/c5d06a922bc2fc4841bc33d0bd7dcc68244ce5ebacfd717aaa710044ec5c3caa.png)",
